<body>
    <h1>Computer Vision Case Study</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <h1 id=3D"ComputerVisionCaseStudy-Introduction">Introduction</h1>
<p>One of the most impactful applications of Computer Vision is in healthca=
re, especially in image-based diagnosis. Detecting nodules in X-rays is a k=
ey task in medicine because these nodules can be signs of serious diseases,=
 like lung cancer. Early detection can really improve treatment and the pat=
ient's overall prognosis. To support doctors and radiologists in this proce=
ss, computer vision technology offers an effective solution.</p>
<p>Create ML makes it possible to build models that automatically analyze X=
-ray images and detect potential lung nodules, making the diagnosis easier =
and speeding up the analysis. In this section, we=E2=80=99ll walk through h=
ow to build one of these solutions using CreateML and Core ML. The app shou=
ld be able to receive and analyze X-rays, identify suspicious areas that mi=
ght contain nodules, and provide automatic pre-diagnoses to radiologists.</=
p>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionCaseStudy-Collecting/CreatingaDataset">Collecting/C=
reating a Dataset</h2>
<p>To train a nodule detection model, it=E2=80=99s necessary to collect a d=
ataset of lung X-rays. This dataset should include both images with nodules=
 and images without them so the model can learn to tell the difference betw=
een the two. You can use public databases like the NIH Chest X-ray dataset =
or LUNA16, or even partner with healthcare institutions to get a diverse an=
d representative dataset.</p>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionCaseStudy-ImageAnnotation">Image Annotation</h2>
<p>Each image in the dataset needs to be annotated, meaning it=E2=80=99s ne=
cessary to manually mark the areas in the images that contain nodules. Thes=
e annotations are essential because they act as labels during the model=E2=
=80=99s training. It=E2=80=99s important that this step is done by experien=
ced radiologists to make sure the model gets accurate information about whe=
re the nodules are.</p>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionCaseStudy-ImagePreprocessing">Image Preprocessing</=
h2>
<p>Before feeding the images into the machine learning model, they need to =
go through preprocessing to ensure they=E2=80=99re in the right format. Pre=
processing can include:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Resizing the images to make sure they=E2=80=99re all the same size.</p><=
/li>
<li>
<p>Normalizing pixel values so the model isn=E2=80=99t affected by differen=
ces in brightness or contrast.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Data augmentation, which applies transformations like rotation or flippi=
ng to increase the diversity of the dataset and make the model more robust.=
</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionCaseStudy-Train-TestSplit"><strong>Train-Test Split=
</strong></h2>
<p>To ensure the model=E2=80=99s accuracy, the dataset needs to be split in=
to training and testing sets before training starts. The training data (usu=
ally around 80% of the total dataset) will be used to teach the model how t=
o identify patterns linked to the presence or absence of nodules, while the=
 test set (usually about 20% of the total dataset) will be set aside to che=
ck how the model performs after it's been trained. It=E2=80=99s really impo=
rtant that the training data is diverse, and in this case, it means includi=
ng a good mix of images with and without nodules. It=E2=80=99s also super i=
mportant that the test data isn=E2=80=99t used during training, so the mode=
l=E2=80=99s ability to handle new, unseen cases can be properly tested.</p>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionCaseStudy-TrainingtheModelwithCreateML">Training th=
e Model with Create ML</h2>
<p>With the data ready, the next step is building and training the model. C=
reate ML has a specific model type called Image Classification. Training wi=
ll be done using the annotated images, and the model will learn to spot the=
 unique features of lung nodules. Once the model is trained, it can be expo=
rted and Core ML can be used to implement it in the solution.</p><span clas=
s=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embed=
ded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=
=3D"imagee.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"132a82d2f4e04a90544a3=
6a9fab7e18913cf59ffbf7c07d8647284bdb1fecf05" data-image-src=3D"https://deve=
loperacademy.atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/1208877192/imagee.png?=
version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1750692276500&amp;cacheVersion=3D1&amp;a=
pi=3Dv2" data-height=3D"1051" data-width=3D"1470" data-unresolved-comment-c=
ount=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1208877206" data-linked-resource-vers=
ion=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-linked-resource-def=
ault-alias=3D"imagee.png" data-base-url=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassi=
an.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png" data-linked-re=
source-container-id=3D"1208877192" data-linked-resource-container-version=
=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"abf564c2-634e-423a-9eed-a5d29528368a" data-media-ty=
pe=3D"file" height=3D"334"></span>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionCaseStudy-ModelEvaluation">Model Evaluation</h2>
<p>After training, it=E2=80=99s crucial to test how accurate the model is u=
sing the test dataset, which wasn=E2=80=99t touched during training. The ev=
aluation should be based on metrics like accuracy, sensitivity, and specifi=
city to make sure the model is really detecting nodules effectively. If the=
 model doesn=E2=80=99t perform well, adjustments can be made, like running =
another training cycle or refining the preprocessing techniques.</p>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionCaseStudy-AppImplementation">App Implementation</h2=
>
<p>Once the model=E2=80=99s been trained and validated, it can be integrate=
d into the app. The app=E2=80=99s interface should let users easily upload =
X-rays and see the areas flagged as potential nodules. It can also show how=
 likely it is that a certain area is a nodule, helping radiologists with th=
eir analysis. Core ML makes this integration easier by letting the model ru=
n directly on Apple devices like iPhones, iPads, and Macs.</p>
<h2 id=3D"ComputerVisionCaseStudy-Conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
<p>By using Apple=E2=80=99s Create ML and Core ML, it=E2=80=99s possible to=
 build an effective app for detecting lung nodules in X-rays. Automating th=
e analysis process not only speeds up diagnosis but also makes it more accu=
rate, helping professionals catch serious diseases like lung cancer early. =
Plus, with ongoing updates and improvements to the model, the app can adapt=
 to the evolving needs of the healthcare field.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
